Make the single-process browser hold everything a phone needs.

Absorbs, in this order: ohos-single-process-discardable.patch, ohos-pdf-single-process.patch, ohos-single-process-commit-checks.patch, ohos-single-process-extensions.patch. Their descriptions follow.

--- ohos-single-process-discardable.patch

Keep one discardable memory allocator for the whole single-process browser.

OHOS phones run single-process with one in-process renderer thread per
render process host. Each RenderThreadImpl installed its own
ClientDiscardableSharedMemoryManager as the process-wide
base::DiscardableMemoryAllocator, and freed it when its host went away
(a closed tab), so the remaining renderers allocated through a dangling
pointer: SIGSEGV in GpuImageDecodeCache::DecodeImageIfNecessary via
DiscardableMemoryAllocator::AllocateLockedDiscardableMemoryWithRetryOrDie,
jumping to string bytes that reused the freed object. Do what the
upstream TODO (crbug.com/503724) asks: the browser's
DiscardableSharedMemoryManager, which lives as long as the process, is the
process-wide allocator, and renderers leave it in place.

--- ohos-pdf-single-process.patch

Let a phone open a PDF without killing the browser.

Opening any PDF on a phone took the whole browser down with it: the viewer's
own page came up, and a moment later the process was gone. Nothing was
logged, because an argument-less CHECK in an official build is a bare
`brk #0` with no message.

pdf::CreateInternalPlugin() asserts three things before it will build the
plugin, and two of them are about the process layout: that this renderer was
started with --pdf-renderer, and that the frame embedding the plugin lives in
another process. Neither holds on a phone, which runs Chromium
single-process -- the shell has no native child processes to spawn there.
--pdf-renderer is added in RenderProcessHostImpl::AppendRendererCommandLine
and nowhere else, and that never runs for an in-process renderer, so
IsPdfRenderer() was false for every PDF anyone has ever opened on a phone.
That assertion is the one that fired; the other would have fired next,
because without out-of-process iframes the embedder frame is local.

So on OHOS both are skipped when the browser is single-process. Nothing is
given away: what keeps an untrusted page from embedding the internal plugin
is IsPdfInternalPluginAllowedOrigin() on the parent frame, which runs before
all of this and is untouched, and so is the assertion that this frame is not
itself one of those trusted origins -- that one says nothing about processes.

IsPdfRenderer() gates one more thing that matters here: the viewer's page is
exempt from the extension default CSP, because PluginResponseWriter writes
inline style and script into it. That exemption also never fired on a phone,
so the same page is exempted by its origin instead.

The viewer's page also needs the window's own width, which it was not
getting -- that fix lives in ohos-phone-viewport.patch, where the rule it
belongs to already is.

Last, the viewer painted on the wrong thread. PaintManager posts its
paint callback to the process main thread, which in a renderer process
is that renderer's main thread -- but single-process makes it the
browser's UI thread, while Blink keeps driving the plugin from the
renderer thread. The two then raced over the plugin's pixel buffer:
UpdateGeometry reallocating it while DoPaint was erasing it, which is a
segfault whenever the timing lines up. It posts to the thread the
PaintManager was built on instead. SkiaBufferReleaseProc has the same
assumption but only runs under kPdfBufferedPaintManager, which is off.

Tablets and 2in1s spawn real child processes and were never affected.

--- ohos-single-process-commit-checks.patch

Stop process-lock checks from killing a single-process browser.

On a phone, opening an extension's page took the whole app down:
"Terminating renderer for bad IPC message, reason 1"
(RFH_CAN_COMMIT_URL_BLOCKED), then SIGTRAP on CrBrowserMain, from
ValidateURLAndOrigin via ValidateDidCommitParams.

A phone runs Chromium single-process, because the platform lets an app
start no other process. The browser, every page, every WebUI and every
extension share that one process, so a process lock or a grant can only
describe what the browser itself may commit -- which is everything. The
checks were refusing a chrome-extension:// page anyway, and a refusal
there is a "bad message", which in single-process mode is a deliberate
crash. Upstream already makes the same exception for WebUI in
CanRequestURL.

So on OHOS in single-process mode:

- CanCommitURL and CanAccessMaybeOpaqueOrigin allow what they would
  otherwise refuse. The second is also what an extension's service
  worker and storage are checked against, which may be why a service
  worker would not start ("No SW").
- RenderFrameHostImpl::CanCommitOriginAndUrl skips the checks that are
  about which process may hold what -- the embedder's, where an
  extension page must be in its extension's process, and
  ChildProcessSecurityPolicy::CanCommitOriginAndUrl. The first version
  exempted only CanCommitURL, and the embedder's check still refused
  the page, which then stayed blank. The checks about the URL and
  origin themselves still run.
- A refused commit is logged with its URL, origin and lock in a release
  build, where the log said nothing about which URL it was.
- A bad message from the in-process renderer is refused and logged
  rather than crashing the app with every tab in it.

Tablets and 2in1s run renderers in processes of their own and none of
this applies to them.

--- ohos-single-process-extensions.patch

Put every extension in the one process a single-process phone has.

With extension pages committing on a phone, uBlock Origin Lite's popup
came up bare: its own five stylesheets unreadable (SecurityError), a
fetch of its own css failing, no icon font, the script stuck in
"loading", and chrome.runtime.sendMessage to its service worker never
answered.

ProcessMap records one extension per renderer process. A phone runs
single-process -- one renderer, inside the browser, holding every page
and every extension -- so whichever extension got there first owned the
process, and every other one was "not in its process". Its page's own
subresources were then cross-renderer loads and refused
(AllowExtensionResourceLoad), and its API context was classified the way
a web page's would be.

On OHOS in single-process mode ProcessMap::Contains now answers that
every extension is in the one process there is. That is the process
check only: whether a resource belongs to the extension, whether it is
web-accessible, and the rest of AllowExtensionResourceLoad run as before.
A refused extension resource is also logged in a release build, with the
URL, process and destination -- in the page it is only a failed fetch.

That made every context an extension had on a web page look like the
extension's own page to the browser: GetMostLikelyContextType asks the
same question and got the same answer, so a content script was taken for
a privileged extension page. In single-process mode a context on an
http(s) URL is now a content script, whatever Contains() says; the
extension's own pages are on its own origin and are not affected.

The same one process also hosts every WebUI page, so it always has WebUI
bindings, and GetMostLikelyContextType, which asks about those first,
classified every extension context in it -- the service worker too -- as
WebUI. Every extension event (tabs.onUpdated, webNavigation.*, ...) then
failed the availability check at dispatch and was dropped silently;
messaging, which does not go through the event router, still worked. In
single-process mode a context with an extension is now classified as the
extension's; WebUI pages, which have none, are still WebUI.

Tablets and 2in1s run renderers in processes of their own and none of
this applies to them.

Generated for Chromium 154.0.8037.51 after chromium-154-harmonyos.patch.
Apply with:

  git -C /path/to/chromium/src apply /path/to/patches/ohos-single-process.patch

